status: published
date: 2026-10-01 09:30
labels: 
desc: 
comments: off
---
![A lake at dawn](media/lake.jpg)

## Hi, I'm Hanbyeol

I'm a designer at Zeus Software, drawing the screens and colours of **NanumOS**. On days off I walk with a camera; in the evenings I cook something simple.

## What this blog promises

- Only photos I took myself
- Only recipes I actually cooked
- No ads, written slowly

> Write down one good thing you saw today, and tomorrow you'll see two.

[Get in touch](/blog/p/contact.html){.btn}
title: Contact
status: published
date: 2026-10-01 09:40
labels: 
desc: 
comments: off
---
## Say hello

- **Email**: hanbyeol@zeussoft.example
- **Using a photo**: non-commercial use is always welcome with a credit
- **Visiting**: the ground-floor lounge at Zeus Software (weekdays, 11 am to 6 pm)

## Questions I get often

1. **Which camera do you use?** Half a mirrorless, half a phone.
2. **Can I scale the recipes?** Yes - adjust the miso and soy sauce a little at a time.
3. **Where can I try NanumOS?** The try-out opens right in your browser.
title: Starting Nanum Notes
status: published
date: 2026-10-01 10:00
labels: daily, NanumOS
desc: Photos, trips, kitchen days and books in one place
comments: on
image: media/dusk.jpg
---
Hi, I'm Hanbyeol. I draw the screens of **NanumOS** at Zeus Software. After a whole day of choosing colours and letters, I keep finding that the sky on the way home is the best textbook there is.

## What you'll find here

- **Photo walks** from alleys, mountains and the sea
- **Kitchen notes**: home recipes with ten ingredients or fewer
- **Trip logs**, from packing lists to places I'd go back to
- **Book notes**, short and honest
- **Design notes** on the colours and shapes we chose for NanumOS

> A good screen is like a good walk: you reach the end without being hurried.

New posts arrive once or twice a week. Comments are always welcome.

[More about this blog](/blog/p/about.html){.btn}
title: A day in an autumn hanok courtyard
status: published
date: 2026-10-12 16:40
labels: travel, photos
desc: An afternoon on the wooden porch under the maples, three photos
comments: on
image: media/hanok.jpg
---
On the reddest weekend of the season I visited an old hanok village. Sitting on the wooden porch, I could hear the wind slip round the eaves and drop into the courtyard.

## The light at four

As the sun lowers, the grain of the roof tiles sharpens and every maple leaf glows as if someone switched it on. Here are two more photos from that day.

![Clouds at dusk](media/dusk.jpg)
![A lake at dawn](media/lake.jpg)

### Getting there

1. Twenty minutes by village bus from the station
2. Ten minutes along the stone-wall lane
3. Pick up a map at the visitor centre

> Tip: go after three in the afternoon - fewer people, the best light.

---

Next time I want to photograph the same courtyard under snow.
title: Walking the cherry road: five photo tips
status: published
date: 2026-10-20 10:05
labels: photos
desc: Simple tips for beginners
comments: on
image: media/cherry.jpg
---
People ask how I take pictures, so here are the five things I do.

1. Shoot when the sun is low (morning, dusk)
2. Keep the horizon level
3. Put the road in the middle so the vanishing point shows
4. Take both landscape and portrait at each spot
5. Take many, with an eraser-like mind

> The last one is honestly the most important.

### Read next

- [Five ways to photograph a city at night](/blog/2026/11/night-city-photo.html)
- [A spring walk album](/blog/2026/10/spring-azaleas.html)
title: A lake at dawn and five books from November
status: published
date: 2026-11-19 07:10
labels: books
desc: Short, honest reading notes for the month
comments: on
image: media/lake.jpg
---
This November I got up early and read on a bench by the lake. Counting how many pages I could read while the mist lifted became a small game.

## This month's five

1. **Jane Eyre**: the red room chapter still gets me every time â˜…â˜…â˜…â˜…â˜…
2. **Pride and Prejudice**: I forgot how fast the dialogue is â˜…â˜…â˜…â˜…â˜†
3. **Hamlet**: one short soliloquy, a whole day of thinking â˜…â˜…â˜…â˜…â˜†
4. **The Velveteen Rabbit**: read it aloud and it becomes a different book â˜…â˜…â˜…â˜…â˜…
5. **Don Quixote**: halfway. I have reached the windmills â˜…â˜…â˜…â˜†â˜†

> Reading is not about finishing fast. It is about what stays.

All five are on the sample shelf of the NanumOS Books app, ready to read.
