title: Picnic kimbap: five rolls that never fail
status: published
date: 2026-11-07 11:20
labels: recipes
desc: Classic kimbap to pack with a bottle of barley tea
comments: on
image: media/kimbap.jpg
---
An autumn picnic means kimbap. Five rolls feed four people generously.

## Ingredients (five rolls)

- 4 bowls of rice, 1 tbsp sesame oil, a pinch of salt
- 5 sheets of kimbap seaweed
- 3 eggs, pickled radish, braised burdock
- A bunch of spinach, 1 carrot
- 2 fish cakes (or ham)

## Method

1. Season the rice with sesame oil and salt and let it cool a little
2. Make a thick omelette and cut it into long strips
3. Blanch the spinach and season it with salt and sesame oil
4. Julienne the carrot and stir-fry it briefly
5. Spread a thin layer of rice on the seaweed, add the fillings, roll it tight

> Tip: a drop of sesame oil on the knife keeps the rolls from tearing.

---

Boil the barley tea the night before and the morning is suddenly relaxed.
title: Sunday miso stew
status: published
date: 2026-11-15 12:30
labels: recipes
desc: A home stew from anchovy stock
comments: on
image: media/stew.jpg
---
My mother taught me to begin with the stock. Keep the order and it never fails.

## Ingredients (serves two)

1. 2 cups anchovy stock
2. 1.5 tablespoons miso paste
3. Half a zucchini, half a block of tofu, half an onion
4. One green chili

## Method

Dissolve the miso in the stock, add the firm vegetables first, tofu last, and take it off the heat after one boil.

> Tip: strain the miso through a sieve for a much cleaner broth.

### Good on the side

- Freshly cooked rice
- A rolled omelette
- A small plate of kimchi
title: Choosing colours for NanumOS: the story of our palette
status: published
date: 2026-11-11 18:00
labels: design, NanumOS
desc: How five colours paint a whole screen
comments: on
image: media/palette.png
---
Colour was one of the things we debated longest while building NanumOS. This blog is painted the same way: five colours, no more.

## Five colours and their jobs

- **Deep navy**: text and headings, the backbone of the screen
- **Coral**: buttons and highlights, where the eye should land first
- **Saffron**: rules and underlines, small joys
- **Teal**: links and sub-headings
- **Sand**: boxes on the background, quotes

## Written as code

```
:root {
  --fg:   #1D3557;  /* deep navy */
  --ac:   #E76F51;  /* coral */
  --gold: #E9C46A;  /* saffron */
  --link: #2A9D8F;  /* teal */
  --card: #F4E9D8;  /* sand */
}
```

![Navy and gold waves](media/wave.jpg)
![Navy and gold facets](media/facets.jpg)

> More colours do not make a page richer. Clear roles do.

### Try it yourself

1. Pick the one text colour you will use most
2. Add one accent that pulls the eye
3. Let the other three bridge the two

[About this blog](/blog/p/about.html){.btn}
title: Five ways to photograph a city at night
status: published
date: 2026-11-02 21:30
labels: photos
desc: The settings that change night shots with one tripod
comments: on
image: media/city.jpg
---
Night scenes feel hard because they are dark and easy to blur, but with a few habits even a phone does well.

## Five ways

1. **Shoot in the blue hour**: twenty minutes after sunset, while the sky is still blue
2. **Use a tripod**, or a railing, or your bag
3. **Two-second timer**, so even your finger does not shake it
4. **Lower the exposure** so the lights do not burn out
5. **Go long**, and car lights turn into ribbons

## Settings from that night

```
Camera        : mirrorless, 24mm
Shutter       : 8 s
Aperture      : f/8
ISO           : 100
White balance : 3800K
```

![A rainy alley](media/alley.jpg)

After rain an alley turns into a mirror and doubles every light. Take an umbrella and go out.

> Patience makes the picture, not the gear.
